Root cause: `No qualifying bean of type 'PasswordEncoder'` — my SecurityConfiguration's `passwordEncoder()` @Bean isn't being created. Why? The SecurityConfiguration bean itself probably failed to load or was skipped!

Hmm — "Database is up to date, no changesets to execute" — Liquibase ran. Then bean creation failed on UserService (needs PasswordEncoder from SecurityConfiguration). If SecurityConfiguration failed to construct or its @Bean methods weren't processed... 

Possible causes:
1. SecurityConfiguration is an @Configuration without @Configuration proxy? I wrote `@EnableWebSecurity @EnableMethodSecurity(...) @Import(SecurityProblemSupport.class) public class SecurityConfiguration` — no @Configuration annotation! In Spring Boot 3, configuration class detection CHANGED: Spring Framework 6.0 removed the "lite" configuration-mode detection?? No wait — classes WITHOUT @Configuration but WITH @Bean methods are "lite" mode (component-scanned as configuration candidates via @Component). Hmm — in Spring 6, `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods=false)`... The class must be picked up by component scanning: @EnableWebSecurity is NOT a @Component stereotype... 

WAIT — the 7.9.3 SecurityConfiguration ALSO had no @Configuration! It had `@EnableWebSecurity @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(...) @Import(...)` — and it worked in Boot 2.7. Because: component scanning picks up ANY class with @Bean methods as "lite" configuration candidate? Spring's ClassPathScanningCandidateComponentProvider checks for @Component OR... hmm — actually @EnableWebSecurity is meta-annotated with... let me think. In Spring 5, `@EnableWebSecurity` is `@Retention(RUNTIME) @Target(TYPE) @Documented @Import(WebSecurityConfiguration.class ...) @EnableGlobalAuthentication @Configuration` — hmm is it meta-annotated @Configuration?? Hmm — if @EnableWebSecurity is meta-annotated with @Configuration... no I don't think so.

Hmm wait — how did 7.9.3 component-scan find SecurityConfiguration then? Spring's component scan (ClassPathScanningCandidateComponentProvider.isCandidateComponent → AnnotationTypeFilter for @Component) — @EnableWebSecurity is not a @Component... 

Hmm — BUT AnnotatedBeanDefinitionReader/ConfigurationClassPostProcessor ALSO processes "lite" candidates: any bean definition whose class has @Bean methods is treated as a lite configuration class IF it's registered as a bean definition. But it must FIRST become a bean definition via component scan. Component scan (by default) includes: classes with @Component OR meta-annotated... @EnableWebSecurity... let me check: Spring Security's @EnableWebSecurity:

```java
@Retention(value = RUNTIME)
@Target(value = TYPE)
@Documented
@Import({ WebSecurityConfiguration.class, SpringApplicationAotRuntimeHintRegistrar.class, ... SpringWebMvcImportSelector.class, OAuth2ImportSelector.class, ... })
@EnableGlobalAuthentication
@Configuration
public @interface EnableWebSecurity {
```

YES — @EnableWebSecurity IS meta-annotated with @Configuration! And @Configuration IS meta-annotated with @Component → component scan picks it up! That's how 7.9.3 worked.

So in Spring Security 6.1.5, is @EnableWebSecurity still meta-annotated @Configuration?? Let me check. If they removed @Configuration from it (I think they did in Spring Security 6 — hmm), then my SecurityConfiguration isn't component-scanned → no passwordEncoder bean → this failure!

And how does the REFERENCE's SecurityConfiguration handle this? IT HAS `@Configuration` explicitly:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity(securedEnabled = true)
public class SecurityConfiguration {
```

YES! The reference added @Configuration (JHipster 8). That's the fix. Also — hmm, wait, the reference has @EnableWebSecurity? Looking at the reference file earlier:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity(securedEnabled = true)
public class SecurityConfiguration {
```

NO @EnableWebSecurity in the reference! Interesting — in Spring Boot 3, SpringBootWebSecurityConfiguration auto-configures the default security filter chain (with securityFilterChain @ConditionalOnMissingBean) — and @EnableMethodSecurity just adds method security. The default Spring Boot security auto-config kicks in (spring-boot-starter-security → SecurityAutoConfiguration → @EnableWebSecurity effectively via SpringBootWebSecurityConfiguration). So the reference relies on Boot's auto-config for the web security part.

For MY config: add `@Configuration` + keep `@EnableWebSecurity` (like the 7.9.3 which had @EnableWebSecurity — it's harmless and explicit — with Boot's auto-config, adding @EnableWebSecurity is also fine/standard).

Fix: add @Configuration to SecurityConfiguration.

